    <string name="tulkki_failures_when_failed">Failed %1$s \u00b7 %2$s</string>
    <string name="tulkki_failures_when_arrived">Arrived %1$s \u00b7 %2$s</string>
    <string name="tulkki_failures_when_send_failed">Send failed %1$s \u00b7 %2$s</string>
    <string name="tulkki_failures_when_written">Written %1$s \u00b7 %2$s</string>
    <string name="tulkki_failures_unknown_conversation">A conversation that is no longer here</string>
    <!--
      A failed send's reason is kept only for the newest failure, so an older one has to say that
      rather than show the last failure's words as if they were its own.
    -->
    <string name="tulkki_failures_reason_not_kept">Why this one failed was not kept</string>
    <string name="tulkki_failures_will_retry">Will be tried again</string>
    <string name="tulkki_failures_gave_up">No more attempts will be made</string>
    <!--
      A send is the owner's, not the queue's: it stays held in the composer until they retry it, so the
      row says what is true of it rather than promising an attempt nothing made.
    -->
    <string name="tulkki_failures_held">Held, not sent</string>
    <!--
      The failures row's own action, and the same meaning the covered bubble's tap carries: translate
      this one message now. The held send's two actions reuse the composer bar's own strings
      (tulkki_hold_send_as_written, and the owner's editable retry wording), because they are the same
      buttons and must not say two different things.
    -->
    <string name="tulkki_failures_translate_now">Translate now</string>

    <!--
      The reading aid's sheet. The labels are the fields of a gloss; the failure captions come from
      the cover's own strings, so a lookup that could not happen says the same thing a covered bubble
      would.
    -->
    <string name="tulkki_gloss_dictionary">Dictionary form</string>
    <string name="tulkki_gloss_ending">Ending</string>
    <string name="tulkki_gloss_case">Case</string>
    <string name="tulkki_gloss_meaning">Meaning</string>
    <string name="tulkki_gloss_nothing">Nothing to look up here.</string>

    <!--
      The two sides of a translated bubble. The first is the one a screen reader hears for the drawn
      second half; the second is what it is told about the half that is never readable. The language
      is filled in from the conversation's resolved language, never written down here.
    -->
    <string name="tulkki_second_half_sent">What was sent, in %1$s</string>
    <string name="tulkki_second_half_original">The original, in %1$s, is not shown</string>

    <!--
      The review's way in: the row in a bubble's own long-press menu, and the title of the surface it
      opens. It names what the notes are about rather than calling them a "review", because the notes
      are about the owner's own wording. The remarks themselves are in the study language and are
      never written down here.
    -->
    <string name="tulkki_review">Notes on your wording</string>

    <!--
      The three instructions, editable. Each row opens the wording that is actually sent, so an owner
      who never edits one sees exactly what the app says on their behalf. The placeholder is %1$s (and
      %2$s in the review, which is the only instruction with two languages in it): the language's name
      is substituted at request time, so an instruction can never be pinned to the language that
      happened to be set when it was written. Clearing a field restores the shipped wording, which is
      why there is no separate "reset" row to drift from it.
    -->
    <string name="tulkki_prompts">Prompts</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompts_summary">What Tulkki sends with every request, and what you can rewrite</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_translate">Translation instruction</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_review">Instruction for the notes</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_gloss">Instruction for a tapped word</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_translate_hint">%1$s is the language the message is translated into. The field opens holding the wording Tulkki ships.</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_review_hint">%1$s is the language the message is translated into; %2$s is the language the notes are written in.</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_gloss_hint">%1$s is the language a tapped word is explained in.</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_shipped">Shipped wording. %1$s</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_edited">Yours, %1$s characters. %2$s</string>
    <string name="tulkki_prompt_clear_hint">Clear this field to go back to the shipped wording.</string>

    <!--
      The recording timer's own tap, which is the whole of the pause control: the tree's timer was a
      bare TextView that paused a live recording and resumed a paused one, and it said nothing to a
      screen reader in either state. The gesture is unchanged; only its name is new, and it names the
      action rather than the bar.
    -->
    <string name="tulkki_recording_pause">Pause recording</string>
    <string name="tulkki_recording_resume">Resume recording</string>
</resources>
